        topic: 'Kinematic chain, mechanism and structure',
        slides: [20, 21, 22],
        terms: ['kinematic chain', 'mechanism', 'structure', 'inversion', 'frame'],
        definition:
          'A kinematic chain is an assembly of links connected by pairs in which no link is yet fixed. Fixing one link converts the chain into a mechanism, and because any link may be fixed, one chain yields as many mechanisms as it has links, each called an inversion. Whether the result moves at all depends on the mobility: if it is one or more the assembly is a mechanism, if it is zero the assembly is a structure that cannot move, and if it is negative the assembly is a statically indeterminate or preloaded structure. The same set of links and pairs may therefore be a useful mechanism or a rigid frame depending only on how many of each there are.',
        takeaway: 'A chain becomes a mechanism when a link is fixed, and whether it moves at all is decided by the mobility count rather than by inspection.',
        visual: 'chain-to-mechanism-inversions',
        visualSpec:
          'A four-link chain drawn floating with no link fixed, then four inversions arranged around it, each with a different link hatched as the frame and the resulting motion sketched: the crank-rocker, the drag link, the double rocker and the inverted case. Beneath, a mobility ladder shows three assemblies of the same links with increasing numbers of joints, giving mobility one, zero and minus one, labelled mechanism, structure and preloaded structure, with the corresponding physical examples of a linkage, a triangulated truss and an overconstrained frame. On reveal: the floating chain appears and flexes freely, each inversion locks a different link and demonstrates its motion, then the mobility ladder builds and each assembly is tested by attempting to move it.',
        algo: [
          'Assemble the links and pairs as a kinematic chain with nothing fixed',
          'Compute the mobility of the chain from its link and joint counts',
          'Fix one link to obtain a mechanism, and note which link was fixed',
          'Repeat with each other link fixed in turn to obtain the inversions',
          'Classify the result as mechanism, structure or preloaded structure from the mobility value',
        ],
        complexity: { best: 'a four-link chain has four inversions, three of which are practically distinct', avg: 'inversions of the slider-crank chain give the oscillating cylinder engine, the Whitworth mechanism and the pendulum pump', worst: 'a chain of mobility zero is a structure and no choice of frame will make it move', note: 'Inversion changes which link is fixed and therefore changes the motion, but it does not change the relative motion between any two links.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'The slider-crank chain has four inversions. What are they?',
          steps: [
            'Fixing the frame gives the ordinary reciprocating engine or pump',
            'Fixing the crank gives the Whitworth quick return mechanism and the rotary engine',
            'Fixing the connecting rod gives the oscillating cylinder engine and the crank and slotted lever mechanism',
            'Fixing the slider gives the hand pump and the pendulum pump',
          ],
          result: 'Four mechanisms of quite different appearance and use, from one chain. The relative motion between any two links is identical in all four; only the frame of reference has changed.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'CHAIN, MECHANISM, STRUCTURE\n\n  KINEMATIC CHAIN  links + pairs, NOTHING fixed yet\n  MECHANISM        a chain with ONE LINK FIXED\n  INVERSION        the mechanism obtained by fixing a\n                   DIFFERENT link. n links -> n inversions.\n\nWHAT MOBILITY DECIDES:\n\n  M >= 1   MECHANISM              it moves\n  M =  0   STRUCTURE              it cannot move\n  M <  0   PRELOADED STRUCTURE    statically indeterminate\n\nFOUR INVERSIONS OF THE SLIDER-CRANK CHAIN:\n\n  fix FRAME       -> reciprocating engine / pump\n  fix CRANK       -> Whitworth quick return, rotary engine\n  fix CONN. ROD   -> oscillating cylinder engine,\n                     crank and slotted lever mechanism\n  fix SLIDER      -> hand pump, pendulum pump\n\n  FOUR very different-looking machines. ONE chain.\n\nKEY POINT:\n  inversion changes WHICH LINK IS FIXED, and so changes\n  the absolute motion. It does NOT change the RELATIVE\n  motion between any two links.\n\nSOURCE: TB2 Ch.1, pp.17-33 (5th ed. supplied).' },
        mistake: 'Students expect an inversion to be a different machine built from different parts. It is the same chain viewed from a different fixed link, which is why the relative motions are unchanged and only the appearance differs.',
        book: { chapter: 1, pages: '17-33' },
        bloom: 'L4',
        co: 'CO1',
