        topic: 'Absolute and relative motion, and vector operations',
        slides: [5, 6, 7],
        terms: ['absolute motion', 'relative motion', 'vector addition', 'vector subtraction', 'reference frame'],
        definition:
          'Absolute motion is motion measured relative to the fixed link, and relative motion is motion measured relative to any other body. Since the fixed link is itself only a link that has been chosen as the reference, the distinction is one of convenience rather than of physics. The whole of velocity and acceleration analysis rests on one relation, that the absolute velocity of a point equals the absolute velocity of a second point plus the velocity of the first relative to the second. Because these are vector quantities, the addition is performed head to tail and the subtraction by reversing the subtracted vector, and a scaled drawing of that addition is the velocity polygon.',
        takeaway: 'Absolute equals absolute plus relative, and because the quantities are vectors the whole method is a drawing exercise.',
