        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'ABSOLUTE AND RELATIVE MOTION\n\n  ABSOLUTE : measured relative to the FIXED link\n  RELATIVE : measured relative to ANY other body\n\n  the fixed link is only a link we CHOSE. So the\n  distinction is convenience, not physics.\n\nTHE ONE RELATION THE WHOLE MODULE USES:\n\n  V_B = V_A + V_BA\n\n  "velocity of B" = "velocity of A" + "velocity of B\n                                      relative to A"\n\n  rearranged:   V_BA = V_B - V_A\n\nVECTOR ARITHMETIC:\n  ADD      : head to tail\n  SUBTRACT : REVERSE the subtracted vector, then add\n\nWORKED: V_A = 3 m/s EAST, V_B = 4 m/s NORTH\n\n  V_BA = V_B - V_A\n       = 4 NORTH + 3 WEST        (reverse the 3 EAST)\n       = sqrt(16 + 9) = 5 m/s\n       at atan(3/4) = 37 deg west of north\n\n  An observer riding ON A sees B doing THAT.\n  Neither absolute velocity is the answer.\n\nSOURCE: TB2 Ch.3, pp.105-111 (5th ed. supplied).' },
