        complexity: { best: 'a pin between the frame and one moving link has a rubbing velocity set by that link alone', avg: 'a pin between two moving links needs the relative angular velocity', worst: 'getting the senses wrong can give a rubbing velocity that is too small by a factor of several', note: 'Rubbing velocity multiplied by the friction force gives the rate of heat generation at the joint, which is what determines bearing life.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'Two links at a pin of 25 millimetre diameter rotate at 12 and 8 radians per second in opposite senses. Find the rubbing velocity.',
          steps: [
            'The senses are opposite, so the relative angular velocity is the sum, that is 20 radians per second',
            'The pin radius is half the diameter, that is 0.0125 metres',
            'Rubbing velocity is the relative angular velocity multiplied by the pin radius',
            'That is 20 multiplied by 0.0125',
          ],
          result: '0.25 metres per second. Had the senses been the same, the relative angular velocity would have been 4 rather than 20 and the rubbing velocity only 0.05 metres per second, a fivefold difference from the sense alone.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'ANGULAR VELOCITY AND VELOCITY OF RUBBING\n\nANGULAR VELOCITY FROM THE POLYGON:\n\n  omega_link = |relative velocity between two of its points|\n               ---------------------------------------------\n               distance between those points\n\n  e.g.  omega3 = |ab| / AB ,  omega4 = |ob| / O4B\n\n  SENSE: apply the relative velocity at one point with the\n  other HELD. Which way does the link swing? That is the sense.\n\nVELOCITY OF RUBBING AT A PIN:\n\n  v_rub = r_pin * omega_relative\n\n  omega_relative = omega_1 + omega_2   OPPOSITE senses\n                 = omega_1 - omega_2   SAME sense\n\n  (for a pin joining a moving link to the FRAME,\n   omega_relative is just that link omega)\n\nWORKED: pin 25 mm dia, links at 12 and 8 rad/s\n\n  OPPOSITE senses: omega_rel = 20 rad/s\n     v_rub = 20 * 0.0125 = 0.25 m/s\n\n  SAME sense:      omega_rel =  4 rad/s\n     v_rub =  4 * 0.0125 = 0.05 m/s\n\n  FIVEFOLD difference, from the SENSE alone.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS:\n  v_rub * friction force = HEAT GENERATED at the joint\n  -> that sets bearing temperature and bearing LIFE.\n\nSOURCE: TB2 Ch.3, pp.111-119 (5th ed. supplied).' },
        mistake: 'Students use the pin diameter rather than the radius, doubling every rubbing velocity, or add the angular velocities without checking the senses, which can be wrong by a factor of several.',
