        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'FOUR-BAR AND SLIDER-CRANK: SAME METHOD\n\n  THE ONLY DIFFERENCE IS THE OUTPUT DIRECTION.\n\n  FOUR-BAR      output pin B moves on a CIRCLE about O4\n                -> V_B is PERPENDICULAR to O4B\n\n  SLIDER-CRANK  the slider moves on a STRAIGHT LINE\n                -> V_slider is ALONG the slide\n\n  everything else is identical:\n    V_A perpendicular to the crank, magnitude omega2*O2A\n    relative velocity perpendicular to the coupler / rod\n    intersect the two known directions\n\nDEAD CENTRES (slider-crank):\n  crank and connecting rod COLLINEAR\n  -> the slider is momentarily at REST\n  -> the polygon degenerates to a LINE\n  -> these are exactly the positions where the PISTON\n     REVERSES: inner and outer dead centre.\n\nWARNING near dead centre:\n  the two construction directions become nearly PARALLEL.\n  The intersection is ILL CONDITIONED - a small drawing\n  error gives a large velocity error. Use the analytical\n  method there instead.\n\nSOURCE: TB2 Ch.3, pp.111-131 (5th ed. supplied).' },
