        definition:
          'A planar vector may be represented by a complex number whose real part is its horizontal component and whose imaginary part is its vertical component, and in polar form it is written as the magnitude multiplied by the exponential of the imaginary unit times the angle. This representation is chosen because multiplying by the exponential of an angle rotates a vector through that angle, so rotation becomes multiplication and the differentiation of a rotating vector becomes trivial. Complex algebra therefore converts the geometry of a linkage into arithmetic that a computer can perform millions of times, which is what makes the analysis of a complete cycle practical.',
        takeaway: 'A complex exponential is a rotation operator, which is why representing links as complex numbers turns geometry into arithmetic.',
