      'Built from TB2 Chapters 7 and 8, which cover the syllabus module almost exactly. The organising idea is that everything about gear tooth geometry follows from one requirement: the angular velocity ratio must be constant. That requirement produces the law of gearing, the law produces the involute, the involute produces the pressure angle and the base circle, and interference and contact ratio are the two practical limits on how far the involute can be pushed. Gear trains are placed last because they are an application of the ratio rather than of the geometry, and epicyclic trains close the module because they are the only part of it where the ratio is not simply a product of tooth numbers. All page numbers are 5th-edition pages; see the edition notice in the file header.',
