        definition:
          'Gears are classified primarily by the relative disposition of the two shafts they connect. Parallel shafts are served by spur gears, whose teeth are parallel to the axis, and by helical gears, whose teeth lie on a helix. Intersecting shafts are served by bevel gears, whose pitch surfaces are cones, in straight, spiral and hypoid forms. Shafts that neither intersect nor are parallel, described as skew or crossed, are served by crossed helical gears and by the worm and worm wheel. Each arrangement carries its own consequences for load capacity, smoothness, thrust and efficiency, and the choice of shaft arrangement therefore determines the gear type before any tooth calculation begins.',
        takeaway: 'The relative position of the two shafts decides the gear type, and the gear type then decides the smoothness, the thrust and the efficiency.',
